---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';

const title = "About — Scarlett Gately Moore";
const description = "About Scarlett Gately Moore — Linux distribution engineer, KDE and Kubuntu contributor, and open-source developer.";
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <style>
      .grid-bg {
        position: fixed;
        inset: 0;
        background-image:
          linear-gradient(rgba(55,138,221,0.04) 1px, transparent 1px),
          linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(55,138,221,0.04) 1px, transparent 1px);
        background-size: 40px 40px;
        pointer-events: none;
        z-index: 0;
      }
      .orb {
        position: fixed;
        width: 600px;
        height: 600px;
        border-radius: 50%;
        background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(24,95,165,0.15) 0%, transparent 70%);
        top: -200px;
        right: -200px;
        pointer-events: none;
        z-index: 0;
      }
      main {
        position: relative;
        z-index: 1;
        max-width: 720px;
        width: calc(100% - 4rem);
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 4rem 0;
        animation: fadeUp 0.6s ease forwards;
      }
      .page-tag {
        font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
        font-size: 11px;
        color: var(--accent);
        letter-spacing: 0.15em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 8px;
      }
      .page-tag::before {
        content: '';
        display: inline-block;
        width: 24px;
        height: 1px;
        background: var(--accent);
      }
      .prose h1 {
        font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
        font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.6rem);
        font-weight: 700;
        color: var(--text);
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
        line-height: 1.2;
      }
      .prose h2 {
        font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
        font-size: 1rem;
        font-weight: 700;
        color: var(--accent);
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
        padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--blue-border);
      }
      .prose p {
        font-size: 16px;
        color: var(--text-muted);
        line-height: 1.8;
        margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
      }
      .prose strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }
      .prose ul {
        list-style: none;
        padding: 0;
        margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 0.75rem;
      }
      .prose ul li {
        font-size: 15px;
        color: var(--text-muted);
        line-height: 1.6;
        padding-left: 1.25rem;
        position: relative;
      }
      .prose ul li::before {
        content: '//';
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
        font-size: 11px;
        color: var(--accent);
        top: 3px;
      }
      .prose a {
        color: var(--accent);
        text-decoration: none;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--blue-border);
        transition: border-color 0.2s;
      }
      .prose a:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
      .contact-grid {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 0.75rem;
        margin-top: 1rem;
      }
      .contact-row {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 1rem;
        padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
        background: var(--bg2);
        border: 1px solid var(--blue-border);
        border-radius: 6px;
        text-decoration: none;
        transition: border-color 0.2s, background 0.2s;
      }
      .contact-row:hover {
        border-color: var(--accent);
        background: var(--bg3);
      }
      .contact-label {
        font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
        font-size: 11px;
        color: var(--accent);
        letter-spacing: 0.05em;
        min-width: 70px;
      }
      .contact-value {
        font-size: 14px;
        color: var(--text-muted);
      }
      @keyframes fadeUp {
        from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(16px); }
        to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="grid-bg"></div>
    <div class="orb"></div>
    <Header />
    <main>
      <div class="page-tag">about</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h1>Hi, I'm Scarlett.</h1>

        <p>
          I'm a technology professional whose career has taken me from systems
          administration to Linux distribution engineering, software packaging, and
          open-source development. I hold a BS in Computer Information Systems
          from DeVry University. These days, I'm also training as a property tax
          appraiser with Apache County, Arizona.
        </p>

        <h2>How I found my way into technology</h2>

        <p>
          My career didn't begin in software or even in IT. I started out as a secretary.
          Somewhere along the way, people noticed I was good at figuring things out and
          saw that I had a knack for technology. That opened the door to a network
          administrator position, and I kept moving forward: first into Linux
          administration, then into systems management.
        </p>

        <p>
          Linux became a big part of that journey. I started back in the days when
          installing Red Hat meant feeding your computer something like ten floppy
          disks. It was a very different world from today's distributions, but I was
          hooked. Learning how systems fit together, solving problems, and making
          technology work became the thread connecting each stage of my career.
        </p>

        <p>
          A move to the East Coast, after my husband was relocated, changed the
          direction of my career. I found it extremely difficult to get hired into
          traditional IT roles there; being a woman in that environment often meant
          that doors simply didn't open. So I adapted and made a pivot into software.
          That change turned out to be the beginning of a new chapter, bringing
          together my systems background and a growing interest in building, packaging,
          and maintaining software.
        </p>

        <h2>The open source side</h2>

        <p>
          Open source has been the thread running through much of my working life. What
          began with KDE documentation in 2012 grew into Linux distribution packaging,
          reproducible builds, continuous integration, release engineering, and years of
          maintaining software and infrastructure that other developers depend on.
          I've worked across the KDE, Kubuntu, Ubuntu, and Debian communities, often
          moving between the application, distribution, and build-system layers to help
          make the whole stack work.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Kubuntu Developer</strong> — contributor to Kubuntu development and member of the Kubuntu Council</li>
          <li><strong>Debian Developer</strong> — an official member of the Debian project with upload privileges, with experience in reproducible builds, KDE/Qt and Python packaging</li>
          <li><strong>KDE infrastructure and CI</strong> — built the first iteration of KDE's Jenkins-based CI at build.kde.org and later worked on build and release infrastructure</li>
          <li><strong>KDE Snaps</strong> — involved from the early days and solo maintainer for much of the project's existence</li>
          <li><strong>Netrunner</strong> — project manager for a community-driven Linux distribution through multiple stable release cycles</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          My work has ranged from packaging individual applications and libraries to
          designing CI systems, maintaining runtimes, and helping plan complete
          distribution platforms. The tools have included Jenkins, GitLab CI, Docker,
          LXD, Snapcraft, AppImage, and multi-architecture build environments across
          AMD64, ARM64, and ARMHF, with development and packaging in Python, Ruby,
          C/C++, and Bash.
        </p>

        <h2>From reproducible builds to Debian</h2>

        <p>
          My early Debian work began through an Outreach program, which led to an opportunity
          with the <strong>Linux Foundation</strong> to work on the emerging reproducible
          builds effort. I helped make a range of software packages reproducible, including
          CMake. The goal was important to the wider free software ecosystem: making it
          possible to independently verify that a source package produces the same binaries
          when built again.
        </p>

        <p>
          As my career moved increasingly toward continuous integration and build
          infrastructure, my Debian work evolved too. I began contributing with the
          Debian KDE and Qt team, and later spent time in Python packaging, including
          work with Mycroft before the company sadly ceased operations.
        </p>

        <p>
          Those different paths eventually led to my becoming an official{" "}
          <strong>Debian Developer</strong>, with upload privileges. My contributions
          there have been quieter in recent years as other professional and community
          commitments have taken priority. I'm still a Debian Developer, and I value
          that part of my open-source journey; I also recognize that I have some
          contribution backlog to work through when time allows.
        </p>

        <h2>Finding my way into Kubuntu</h2>

        <p>
          My KDE story and my Kubuntu story began side by side. After my first KDE
          documentation contributions in 2012, <strong>Jonathan Riddell</strong> — one
          of the people whose work made Kubuntu possible — approached me and asked
          whether I'd be interested in helping with the project. I said yes, and that
          invitation began my journey into Linux packaging.
        </p>

        <p>
          It was an especially interesting time to join. KDE was breaking up the
          monolithic kdelibs codebase into the smaller, modular KDE Frameworks, and I
          helped package that major transition for Kubuntu and get the work integrated
          into Ubuntu. That was my introduction to the depth and complexity of
          distribution packaging: not just building software, but making a large,
          interconnected stack work reliably for users.
        </p>

        <p>
          In those early days, Kubuntu had a remarkable team. We worked hard, built
          things together, and for a while became a familiar part of the wider Linux
          ecosystem. I was a significant part of the original team that went on to create
          KDE neon: a new distribution bringing the latest KDE software to users on a
          stable Ubuntu LTS foundation, with continuous integration driving its builds
          and integration. Being part of that team and helping bring the original OS
          into existence was a defining part of my KDE and Kubuntu years.
        </p>

        <p>
          Over time, the team went in different directions, moving on to different
          projects and careers. KDE Neon is still alive today, sustained by a very
          small volunteer effort. I'm no longer part of its day-to-day maintenance,
          but the work we did together remains an important part of my history in KDE
          and Ubuntu.
        </p>

        <h2>Returning to Kubuntu</h2>

        <p>
          My Kubuntu story didn't end with those early years. I was later brought back
          for the Noble release. It was a chance to return to a project that had been
          a formative part of my career, and to contribute again as a Kubuntu Developer.
        </p>

        <p>
          More recently, <a href="https://kfocus.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kubuntu Focus</a> brought me on for the Resolute LTS release and the early stages of the
          Bulletproof Linux work. That work has since been taken over by{" "}
          <a href="https://techpaladinsoftware.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Techpaladin</a>,
          a new company formed by members of the Blue Systems team. I don't work for
          Techpaladin, but I wanted to acknowledge the team: they're good people, and
          I wish them well as they carry the work forward.
        </p>

        <h2>Growing KDE Snaps from an idea</h2>

        <p>
          KDE Snaps have been one of the longest-running and most hands-on parts of my
          open-source work. I was at the conference where Canonical introduced the Snap
          concept. After the presentation, I approached the speaker and asked whether it
          would be okay to take that concept and use it to create Snaps for KDE applications.
          They were enthusiastic about the idea, and that conversation helped set the KDE
          Snaps effort in motion — a project that has stayed with me ever since.
        </p>

        <p>
          I've been involved with KDE Snaps from those early days and have been their
          solo maintainer for much of their existence. That has meant far more than
          packaging applications: it has meant keeping the infrastructure working,
          solving build and runtime problems, adapting to changes in Snapcraft and the
          KDE stack, and doing the persistent maintenance that keeps software available
          to users. A lot of that work has happened behind the scenes, one bug, build,
          and release at a time.
        </p>

        <p>
          Much of this has been volunteer work — a substantial investment of time,
          energy, and plenty of blood, sweat, and tears — with the occasional short-term
          contract along the way. I'm especially glad that the work is being funded
          now. Being paid for it is a welcome change, and a meaningful recognition of
          the years of effort that went into building and sustaining it.
        </p>

        <p>
          The work is still evolving. I'm currently focused on the next generation of
          KDE Snap infrastructure, including KDE Neon support in Snapcraft and the
          move toward Core26 runtimes. It's a continuation of that original conversation:
          taking an idea and doing the practical, sometimes difficult engineering needed
          to make it real and keep it going.
        </p>

        <h2>Developing the KDE Neon Core vision</h2>

        <p>
          My work with KDE has also extended into product and business planning. I developed
          a full business plan for <strong>KDE Neon Core</strong> — a complete operating
          system initiative built on Ubuntu Core, distinct from the existing KDE Neon
          desktop distribution. Although I didn't ultimately lead the project myself,{" "}
          <a href="https://www.enioka.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">enioka</a>{" "}
          took up my proposal and brought it forward, helping deliver the work for Core24.
        </p>

        <p>
          When enioka was engaged again for Core26, they invited me back to contribute.
          It's been rewarding to see the KDE Neon Core vision continue to evolve, and to
          be part of the next stage of that work.
        </p>

        <h2>Community involvement</h2>

        <p>
          Community has been an important part of my open-source work. I've served two
          terms on the <strong>Ubuntu Community Council</strong>, first around 2015 and
          then returning for a second term more recently. I'm also a member of
          <strong> KDE e.V.</strong> and the <strong>Kubuntu Council</strong>, staying
          involved in the communities and projects that have shaped my career.
        </p>

        <h2>Finding my place in open source</h2>

        <p>
          My first open-source conference was in 2014. In those early days, I was often
          one of the only women in the room. It wasn't always easy navigating that
          environment, but I found my place through the work, the people who became
          friends and collaborators, and a shared passion for building better software.
          I'm genuinely happy to see the community becoming more diverse and welcoming,
          and I hope the next generation of women entering open source finds more doors
          open than I did.
        </p>

        <h2>The day job</h2>

        <p>
          In a bit of a career pivot, I'm now venturing into <strong>property appraisal
          with Apache County, Arizona</strong> as a Property Appraiser I. Arizona's dual
          valuation system, legal classifications, assessment ratios, and the three
          approaches to value keep things interesting. I'm enjoying learning an entirely
          new discipline from the ground up.
        </p>

        <p>
          St. Johns is a long way from a Jenkins server, but the systematic thinking
          translates surprisingly well.
        </p>

        <h2>This blog</h2>

        <p>
          This is my engineering notebook: a place for practical write-ups about Linux, KDE,
          Kubuntu, Snapcraft, packaging, CI/CD, and the occasional lesson from working
          across two very different disciplines.
        </p>

        <h2>Get in touch</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="contact-grid">
        <a class="contact-row" href="mailto:sgmoore@debian.org">
          <span class="contact-label">email</span>
          <span class="contact-value">sgmoore@debian.org</span>
        </a>
        <a class="contact-row" href="https://github.com/scarlettgatelymoore" target="_blank">
          <span class="contact-label">github</span>
          <span class="contact-value">github.com/scarlettgatelymoore</span>
        </a>
        <a class="contact-row" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/scarlettgatelymoore" target="_blank">
          <span class="contact-label">linkedin</span>
          <span class="contact-value">linkedin.com/in/scarlettgatelymoore</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>
